      "id": 1,
      "text": "Marysville was platted in 1871 with 40 lots straddling the Ohio and Mississippi Railroad, later the Baltimore & Ohio line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Mary Kimberlin, daughter of early settler Jacob Kimberlin, whose family helped establish the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/marysville_indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Marysville’s early economy relied on shipping local stock and produce, supported by a general store, sawmill, and cooper‑shop by 1882.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/marysville_indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s post office opened in 1870, followed by a two‑room schoolhouse in the early 1900s and a church built in 1901.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
